WEST, the Business Group Companies, and Affiliates appear in two distinct organizational/contractual frameworks, but in both they help define who is inside the relevant business structure and how intra-group or related-party dealings are treated.

## 1. WEST’s role in organizational structure

WEST is the central named entity within the “Serviced Group” structure.

- “WEST and each other Person within the Serviced Group” are grouped together as the internal side of the servicing relationship.
- WEST has specific notice, cooperation, and control responsibilities, including:
  - cooperating with the Servicer and providing powers of attorney as needed;
  - notifying the Servicer about asset documents, accounts, trustees, and bankruptcy-related actions of persons within the Serviced Group;
  - forwarding communications relating to assets;
  - paying servicing fees to the Servicer;
  - approving or directing certain asset purchases and other transactions.

The Serviced Group is defined as a collection of “Persons within the Serviced Group,” with WEST functioning as the lead or coordinating entity. WEST also represents the group in amendment and approval mechanics; for example, the servicing agreement may be amended only in writing signed by WEST and the Servicer.

## 2. Separation of WEST/Serviced Group from the Servicer and its Affiliates

A major structural feature is separateness.

Each Person within the Serviced Group covenants to remain a separate and readily identifiable business from, and independent of, the Servicer and any of the Servicer’s Affiliates. That separateness includes maintaining:

- separate legal existence and corporate formalities;
- separate assets and liabilities;
- separate books, records, accounts, and minutes;
- separate funds;
- business conducted in its own name;
- no reliance on the Servicer’s creditworthiness;
- no assumption of the Servicer’s debts, except express indemnity obligations.

The Servicer gives mirror-image separateness covenants with respect to the Serviced Group. It must remain separate from each Person within the Serviced Group, keep funds uncommingled, and avoid holding itself out as a division of WEST or its subsidiaries.

Thus, in the WEST structure, Affiliates matter primarily as a boundary concept: the Serviced Group must be independent from the Servicer and the Servicer’s Affiliates, even though consolidated financial statements or consolidated tax filings may still occur if required by law or GAAP.

## 3. Affiliate as a structural concept

“Affiliate” is defined broadly as a person or entity directly or indirectly controlling, controlled by, or under common control with another person or entity.

That definition is used to map organizational relationships:

- in the Serviced Group arrangements, references to the Servicer’s Affiliates identify entities from which the Serviced Group must remain distinct;
- bankruptcy-notification provisions also refer to actions involving a person in the Serviced Group or “any of its Affiliates,” showing that affiliate relationships extend the operational perimeter for certain notices and risk-monitoring;
- confidentiality provisions permit disclosure by the Servicer to its representatives and Affiliates for purposes of the agreement, subject to confidentiality restrictions.

So Affiliates function as legally connected entities that expand the relevant organizational family, but they are also the benchmark for deciding whether a transaction is “non-affiliated” and therefore arm’s-length.

## 4. Business Group Companies as the operating corporate group

The Business Group Companies are a separate collective of the Company and its subsidiaries.

They are treated as a unified business group for operational and compliance representations, including:

- compliance with Food and Drug Law;
- data protection and cybersecurity compliance;
- anti-corruption compliance;
- insurance;
- title to assets;
- customer and supplier relationships;
- material contracts;
- litigation and environmental matters;
- internal accounting controls.

This group concept defines the internal corporate perimeter for disclosures and representations. The Business Group Companies are assessed “taken as a whole” in many provisions, showing that they are treated as an integrated enterprise for transaction diligence and legal compliance.

## 5. How Affiliates affect related-party treatment for Business Group Companies

For the Business Group Companies, Affiliates are crucial to defining related-party exposure.

The agreement states that, except as disclosed, there are no loans, leases, contracts, or other transactions between:

- the Company or any of its subsidiaries, on one hand, and
- officers, directors, direct or indirect equity holders (including any Seller Group Party), employees, or their respective Affiliates or immediate family members, on the other hand.

These are collectively defined as “Related-Party Transactions.”

Related-Party Transactions expressly exclude:
- offer letters,
- employment or similar agreements,
- Company Plans,
- director or officer indemnification agreements.

Material contracts also specifically include “any Contract involving a Related-Party Transaction,” showing that related-party dealings are elevated for disclosure and diligence purposes.

Thus, in the Business Group Companies framework, Affiliates broaden the set of persons whose transactions may be treated as related-party transactions. The concept is not limited to direct insiders; it extends to their affiliated entities and family-connected relationships.

## 6. Arm’s-length treatment across both frameworks

Both frameworks use affiliation to distinguish permissible market-based dealings from suspect intra-group dealings.

### In the WEST / Serviced Group framework:
- transactions between the Servicer and any Person within the Serviced Group may not be “as a whole materially more favorable to either party” than an arm’s-length transaction with a non-affiliated third party;
- transactions entered into by the Servicer on behalf of the Serviced Group must be on an arm’s-length basis and on market terms.

### In the Business Group Companies framework:
- contracts involving related parties are separately identified and disclosed;
- affiliate relationships are part of the definition of who counts as a related party.

So Affiliates serve as the contrast class against which arm’s-length, non-affiliated treatment is measured.

